+    23	## What got done
+    24	
+    25	### #10 — the full review panel (pushed, `3517c17`)
+    26	
+    27	Before: dispatch ran ONE reviewer, and "at least two different model families must
+    28	agree" lived in a config file that nothing enforced.
+    29	
+    30	Now: reviewers run **in parallel against one frozen version**; the **database itself**
+    31	refuses to complete a job unless enough genuinely different model families passed *that
+    32	exact version*; a reviewer that was capped out, switched off or crashed is **written
+    33	down as not having participated** and can never count as approval; money is reserved
+    34	before a paid reviewer is called; and if the panel can't reach the floor, the work waits.
+    35	
+    36	Proved live twice: the free local model built something, Claude and GPT reviewed the
+    37	same version side by side, the job completed, Grok logged as sitting out.
+    38	
+    39	Reviewers found eight real defects in it, including one that would have certified work
+    40	nobody reviewed and one where a broken login would have permanently condemned good code.
+    41	All fixed with regression tests.
+    42	
+    43	### #11 — the gatekeeper (built, NOT pushed, `66ad8a2`)
+    44	
+    45	`harness/gatekeeper.py`. Three verbs — merge, deploy, spend — and no fourth. Agents ask;
+    46	it re-reads the record itself rather than believing the request. `dispatch.ship()`, which
+    47	was one import away from every agent, is gone.
+    48	
+    49	Reviewers hit it hard and found more, all fixed — most seriously that **merge merged
+    50	whatever the branch currently pointed at, never checking it was the code that had been
+    51	reviewed**. Build v1, get it approved, push v2, ask to merge: v2 went in. It now refuses
+    52	unless the branch tip *is* the reviewed version, and the record decides before git is
+    53	touched at all.
+    54	
+    55	### Migration 007 — two holes under #10, found after it was pushed
+    56	
+    57	1. **A job with no requirements had no requirements to fail.** Both completion guards
+    58	   only fired when the requirement counts were above zero, and they default to zero. A
+    59	   job created outside dispatch went straight to done with no reviews at all.
+    60	2. **The builder's own family counted toward "different minds."** A GPT seat could build
+    61	   it, a GPT seat could pass it, and that counted as one of the two required families.
+    62	   Same-family review is still *allowed* (deliberate earlier decision, and banning it
+    63	   outright would deadlock a Claude-built job) — it just no longer counts toward
+    64	   diversity.
